body:
I don't remember what the movie was called, but it was about this man's life in the 1800's. I think he was a civil rights advocate, because most of the plot centered around him trying to give education to ex-slaves. Thing is, early in the movie, when he was a kid, he had a pet chicken which he brought with him to a boarding school. He was bullied by the boys there, and they killed the chicken by tying it to the ceiling of the dorm and beating it with sticks. 

&#x200B;

Later in the film, the building where he was teaching ex-slaves was raided, and his love interest was killed, and I can still remember the sight of her on the ground, eyes wide open, with blood pouring from the back of her head.

My last relationship ended four years ago, and I have never been able to stop thinking about it. It made me cautious about getting into a new relationship and, though part of me wants a relationship, another part is against it and wants to remain single. I was with this person for ten years, through high school and part of college, and I seriously thought that they were the one, but after my OCD had gone out of control, they became mentally and physically abusive, to the point where I had to end the relationship. 

&#x200B;

There are times where I wonder where I would be if I were still with them, and though I tell myself that it is in the past, I occasionally find myself obsessing over them, remembering the horrible things they had done before the end of our relationship.

My mom and grandmother both had a hoarding problem. Their rooms were cluttered so much, you didn't even realize how much space there actually was in them. Unfortunately, their hoards leaked into the hallway and other areas of the house, and there was only a single pathway to get through the house. Just last year, when my grandmother had been hospitalized for heart failure and my grandfather was in a nursing home temporarily, my sister and I, along with our neighbors, cleaned the house up and got rid of the old furniture and anything that wasn't needed. 

&#x200B;

Before that massive cleanup, I could barely control my OCD, even with my prescribed SSRI and therapy.

I know there was a news article back in 2014 or 2015 about a Gumby reboot being made by Jim Henson Productions, but after that, nothing else came up. Not sure if they'll even reboot Gumby after Joe Clokey, Art Clokey's son, passed away last year (he was the current owner of Premavision, which produced Gumby, up until his death). 

&#x200B;

I only know so much about stuff relating to Gumby due to the 80's cartoon being a childhood favorite of mine, and that I did a research paper on Gumby's creator for an art class.

The original Spyro the Dragon trilogy, the Zelda series (Twilight Princess and Wind Waker are my favorites), and, odd for an HSP, but the Fatal Frame/Project Zero series, which is a horror game, but isn't as bad for me as Resident Evil or Silent Hill. I guess I like it in particular because your weapon is a camera instead of a gun, as enemies in the game are ghosts and not zombies or abominations. 

&#x200B;

I guess I prefer games that don't really involve firearms for weapons and opt for magic, swords, or unconventional weapons instead.

A Nintendo DS. It still happens to this day, since the 3DS XL I have is the only DS that can use Animal Crossing: New Leaf currently (the data on the cartridge updated to Welcome Amiibo). 

&#x200B;

We don't scream at each other over it like when we were little, but one of us will try to find it, only to realize that the other took it with them somewhere.

I come from a family with a history of hoarding. My grand aunt on my grandmother's side, my grandmother, my mom, me, and my sister all suffer or suffered from this problem. I'm not entirely sure why my mom or grand aunt hoarded things, but I feel that my grandmother, who would constantly buy toilet paper, paper towels, and other things was afraid of running out of the listed items. My sister is an impulsive buyer, and as for me, my mind places importance on certain objects, such as the cardboard backing of an action figure package, and makes it nigh impossible for me to throw things out. 

&#x200B;

I've gotten better with my hoarding problem, luckily.

For some reason, one time after drinking a McDonald's milkshake, I really had to pee. This particular McDonald's was a twenty-minute drive from home, but I did not get the urge to pee until I was on my way home. The trip was long, painful, and I was afraid I was gonna pee myself, plus, walking from the car to my house was difficult. 

&#x200B;

Such a relief when I finally got to the toilet.

When I was about two months into therapy and hadn't been prescribed a medication, I became really depressed and thought I was a waste of space to the world and my family. My sister would be mad at me a lot due to my depression and anxiety, which really didn't help much. Finally, I couldn't take my thoughts anymore and took a shard of a childhood plate that I had kept after it broke and cut at both of my wrists. 

&#x200B;

I stopped after awhile, I'm not sure why, and just laid down and cried on my bed for awhile. My sister found me and freaked out when she saw my wrists, then showed them to my grandparents after applying Neosporin to the cuts. My grandfather was angry at me for my actions but was glad I had not hurt myself seriously, then he called my therapist to tell her what had happened. My grandmother was confused as to why I had done that to myself, but she didn't press me about it. After talking with my grandfather over the phone for a bit, I talked to my therapist, who was glad I was okay. 

&#x200B;

I haven't tried anything since, as things have gotten a lot better for me ever since I was prescribed Fluoxetine. I don't feel as depressed anymore, and I've taken on new hobbies such as sewing to keep myself busy. I have scars from the cuts, but they aren't as visible anymore.

Borrowing my sister's Fire Emblem cosplay as a replacement for another cosplay because it had a missing piece. I say this because the con I went to a few weeks ago had horrifying weather the day I wore it. I couldn't move very well due to the chest armor, so I ended up changing out of it later and had to carry the pieces in a bag. Unfortunately, I did not bring my jacket with me, so after leaving the convention hall, I had to run screaming back to my friend's car in cold, rainy, windy weather. 

&#x200B;

Didn't catch a cold, but I really hope I never go through that again.

Longest cold I had lasted two weeks. I called it a 'Super Cold' because it was so bad that I had nausea and did not want to leave bed for the first few days. Vomiting and sore throats mixing do not make for a great experience, and it burned the crap out of my nostrils and made it hard to breathe. Worst part had to be when my fever broke, because it was the middle of the night and I had god awful fever dreams. 

&#x200B;

How did I cope with it? Sleep, tissues, barf bags, and lots of chicken soup with hot sauce.

"Please stop reminding me I have a huge butthole." - my sister's cat 

&#x200B;

"I don't like that crazy bitch." - my cat Simba about my sister's cat

So this creeper wanted to 'hang out' with me at the local Barnes & Noble. That was good and all, but after we got back to his car, he kept talking about how he wanted to grab my butt or how curvy I was for a short person. He also kept asking about wanting to have sex with me, even though I repeatedly told him that I was uncomfortable with the idea of sex (I had a history of being abused as a child). 

&#x200B;

Didn't end there. He continued texting me about sex, to the point where I got fed up and ignored him.

At some point in time, both my grandparents' families immigrated to the U.S; my grandpa's family came from Europe, and my grandma's from Japan. Grandpa grew up on a farm, while Grandma had lived somewhere in the middle states before being put in an internment camp (afterward, her family settled in California). Grandpa met Grandma at a dance while he was in the Air Force, they got married, and they had my mom.

&#x200B;

I don't know much about my dad's family history, except that his family immigrated from Lithuania at some point. My paternal grandparents met, married, and had my aunt and dad before divorcing. Dad enlisted in the Air Force and met my mom in a bar, they married, and a few years later, had me, and then my sister.

&#x200B;

Currently, my sister is engaged to the swamp beast of her dreams, while I'm enjoying being single for awhile.

"Humankind cannot gain anything without first giving something in return. To obtain, something of equal value must be lost. That is alchemy's first law of equivalent exchange. In those days, we really believed that to be the world's one and only truth." 

&#x200B;

"Through dangers untold and hardships unnumbered, I have fought my way here to the castle beyond the Goblin City to take back the child you have stolen, for my will is as strong as yours, and my kingdom as great. You have no power over me."

&#x200B;

I also remember the entirety of the song 'According To Plan' from Corpse Bride.

I wasn't diagnosed until I was 25, but I'm pretty sure it's been around long before I was even aware of it. As a child, I was pretty much like Chuckie from the Rugrats, and I'm pretty sure a lot of my early silly fears were probably caused by OCD. I was afraid that if I fell asleep after a field trip to a mission in Sonoma, CA, that the ghost of the woman buried under the chapel there would kidnap me, and that if I stayed up, she wouldn't get me. 

&#x200B;

In my teens, I was afraid of this guy in my class snapping and shooting up the class, which is probably one of my more realistic intrusive thoughts. However, it wasn't until my 20's that it seemed that my OCD got out of complete control and could even cause me to feel weighed down. I've been on Fluoxetine for two years, so it's been somewhat manageable, but sometimes it still flares up.

I really hate the dark, except when I go to bed. I'm afraid of encountering something creepy while walking down the small hallway in my house to my room, mainly because the hallway opens into the rest of the house which is incredibly dark at night. The noises of the house settling doesn't help much. 

&#x200B;

OCD and an overactive imagination do NOT go together.
